{"prdMarkdown":"# Product Requirements Document (PRD): Automated Session Deletion (30-Day Retention)\n\n## Problem\nIn Spec Clarity, sessions and their associated artifacts accumulate in PostgreSQL without a retention limit. Over time, stale session data increases storage overhead, risks degrading query performance across session lists, and creates unnecessary data liability for abandoned feature-spec drafts.\n\n## Goals\n- Automatically purge sessions older than 30 days without requiring manual administrative intervention.\n- Maintain predictable database storage growth by executing periodic scheduled cleanup passes.\n- Ensure all associated session data (questions, documents, pipeline outputs) are handled cleanly without leaving orphaned records.\n\n## Non-Goals\n- Providing an on-demand administrative HTTP trigger or manual deletion UI (confirmed out-of-scope during clarification).\n- End-user configurable retention windows or per-workspace custom retention policies.\n- A user-facing \"trash can\" or self-service recovery mechanism for deleted sessions.\n\n## User Impact\n- Users attempting to revisit session URLs older than the 30-day threshold will receive a 404 / not-found response.\n- Active sessions younger than 30 days will experience zero functional disruption or performance degradation during background cleanup cycles.\n- Depending on whether the cutoff is based on session creation or last modification, inactive workflows left open across 30 days may expire.\n\n## Open Product Questions\n- `[PRODUCT_DECISION]` Should the 30-day retention window be measured against session creation date (`created_at`) or the timestamp of last user update/activity (`updated_at`)?\n- `[PRODUCT_DECISION]` Should the automated cleanup mechanism also remove cloned repository working directories and temporary filesystem artifacts created by `src/pipeline/repoReader.js`, or is this strictly limited to database records?\n- `[ASSUMPTION_MADE]` Data model impact: Because the clarifying question regarding hard vs. soft delete was skipped, we assume permanent hard deletion is the intended default for storage reclamation unless compliance audits dictate soft deletion.\n- `[ASSUMPTION_MADE]` Explicit out-of-scope boundaries: Because the response to temporary clone cleanup was \"Not sure, hadn't thought about that,\" filesystem purge is assumed deferred to a secondary enhancement unless storage audits indicate immediate disk exhaustion risk.","trdMarkdown":"# Technical Requirements Document (TRD): Automated Session Deletion (30-Day Retention)\n\n## Architecture Notes\n- **Database Layer & Cascading Deletions (`src/db/schema.sql`, `src/db/migrate.js`)**:\n  - If hard deletion is employed, `src/db/schema.sql` must enforce `ON DELETE CASCADE` across all foreign key relationships referencing the `sessions` table (such as clarification questions, blast radius results, and generated PRD/TRD documents). Otherwise, delete transactions will throw foreign key constraint violations.\n  - If soft deletion (`deleted_at`) is adopted instead, existing query logic in `src/routes/sessions.js` will require refactoring to include an active-record filter (`WHERE deleted_at IS NULL`) across all fetch endpoints.\n- **Job Scheduling & Process Management (`src/server.js`, `package.json`)**:\n  - Currently, `package.json` contains no background job scheduler (e.g., `node-cron`, `bree`, or `agenda`).\n  - Cleanup execution must be triggered either via a native `setInterval` loop in `src/server.js` or via a dedicated lightweight scheduling package.\n- **Multi-Instance Concurrency & Advisory Locks**:\n  - When scaling the service across multiple containers or instances (e.g., via `docker-compose.yml`), multiple `src/server.js` instances will attempt to run the background job simultaneously.\n  - To prevent table lock contention and redundant transaction work, the deletion job should acquire a PostgreSQL advisory lock (e.g., `SELECT pg_try_advisory_lock(...)`) before proceeding with the batch delete.\n- **Disk & Artifact Management (`src/pipeline/repoReader.js`)**:\n  - If temporary Git repositories or diff caches are stored on the host filesystem during analysis in `src/pipeline/repoReader.js`, deleting database rows alone will leave unlinked disk clutter, defeating the objective of bounded storage.\n\n## Edge Cases & Dependencies\n- `package.json`: Lacks a dedicated cron/scheduling dependency; engineering must decide whether to introduce a library or rely on Node.js native timers with error-handling wrappers.\n- `src/server.js`: Multi-instance deployments could execute overlapping scheduled jobs simultaneously without a distributed concurrency lock against Postgres.\n- `src/db/schema.sql`: Session hard-deletions will fail if child tables lack `ON DELETE CASCADE` constraints.\n- `src/routes/sessions.js`: Adopting a soft-delete pattern will leak expired sessions into user-facing lists unless all queries in `src/routes/sessions.js` are updated with active-record checks.\n- `src/pipeline/repoReader.js`: Local working directories cloned for repo inspection will become orphaned on disk if database records are deleted without a corresponding filesystem cleanup hook.\n- Evaluation timestamp: Deleting based on `created_at` rather than `updated_at` risks terminating active, slowly iterated specs that were initiated over 30 days ago.\n\n## Engineering Questions\n- `[ENGINEERING_DECISION]` Should expired sessions be permanently hard-deleted with cascading foreign keys (`ON DELETE CASCADE`), or soft-deleted via a `deleted_at` column requiring query-level filtering across `src/routes/sessions.js`?\n- `[ENGINEERING_DECISION]` What scheduling and concurrency mechanism should be introduced in `src/server.js` and `package.json` (e.g., an in-process timer with PostgreSQL advisory locks versus an external cron container or CLI command)?\n\n## Estimate\n- **Estimated Effort**: 2-4 days (Standard implementation).\n- **Worst-Case Estimate**: Up to 9 days (if schema migrations require retrofitting foreign keys with cascading rules across all dependent tables, updating all endpoints in `src/routes/sessions.js` for soft-deletion filters, and implementing asynchronous disk cleanup for `src/pipeline/repoReader.js`)."}